@namespace FootballFormation.UI.Components

@* What a page that declares @rendermode InteractiveServer needs and no longer inherits, because
   MainLayout renders statically for every page and RouteView applies it outside the island. Every
   such page renders one of these as its first element. *@

@* The three MudBlazor providers that only work inside an interactive render mode. A render mode
   cannot be put on a layout at all — @Body is a RenderFragment and Blazor cannot serialise one as a
   root component parameter — so left where they were they would be static, and known_issues.md
   records what that looks like: dialogs that simply never appear.

   MudThemeProvider stays in the layout on purpose. MudBlazor separated theming from the popover
   provider precisely so it can live in a static one, and the theme has to reach the whole page. *@
<MudPopoverProvider />
<MudDialogProvider />
<MudSnackbarProvider />

@* And, on the admin-only pages, the gate that boots a session revoked while its owner is looking at
   the app. AuthorizeRouteView still guards the route, but it lives in the static router, so it is
   evaluated once per request and hears nothing afterwards — where the revalidating
   AuthenticationStateProvider goes on checking for as long as the circuit lives. This is the same
   NotAuthorized branch, inside the island, so it still hears it. The redirect is also the request
   that finally clears the auth cookie: a circuit has no response to clear it on.

   Only where the page says so. The public pages need no gate — losing the role there re-renders
   their AuthorizeView blocks into the visitor view, which is the correct outcome, and redirecting a
   visitor who was never signed in would be plainly wrong. *@
@if (AdminOnly)
{
    <AuthorizeView Roles="@AppRoles.Admin">
        <NotAuthorized>
            <RedirectToLogin />
        </NotAuthorized>
    </AuthorizeView>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Mirrors the page's own <c>[Authorize(Roles = AppRoles.Admin)]</c>. Two statements of one
    /// fact, a line apart in the same file, because the attribute is read by the router and this is
    /// read inside the island — and only the island is still listening once the page is up.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool AdminOnly { get; set; }
}
